      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Inside the container, or an address we point at: this is the first card because it changes every other one. An embedded RAD needs no key, no TLS and no traffic leaving the machine \u2014 and it dies with the container. An addressed RAD lives through restarts, can be as big as it likes, and is somebody's server to look after. Decide whether we build one shape or both. If both, write the rule for choosing. #interview ^c-shape**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L71`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 2850
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "868139c79238bfcd",
      "line_num": 72,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Keeping a RAD out of ordinary chat: a RAD arrives as an OpenAI-style connection, so its models land in `app.state.MODELS` and show up in every reader's model picker. That invites exactly the base agent use we already tried and dropped. Three ways out: hide connection models from the picker, mark a model as Space-only, or leave them and tell people not to. The code does neither of the first two today. #interview ^c-picker**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L72`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 2940
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "5deec22bbce9658d",
      "line_num": 73,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**One Sprig, more than one of it: `CATALOG` is keyed by name ([supervisor.py:222](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/supervisor.py#L222)) and `SPRIG_WIRES` is `{name: {wire: value}}` ([wiring.py:119](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/wiring.py#L119)). One entry means one graft and one set of settings. Alexander's direction, 2026-08-09: bake in that some kinds of Sprig can have more than one instance, as a property of the kind. That reaches the published Sprig and Rootstock specs, so this card changes a spec and not only code. Not a blocker, because we hand out pairs \u2014 it is what makes the development pair and the bridge below possible. #interview ^c-many**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L73`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3030
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "0eaec060784dbfda",
      "line_num": 74,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Can agents talk to each other, or do we only think they can?: the belief is that once a RAD is in a Space, `@name` works, and a RAD and a simple agent can talk to each other. The mention scan at [spaces.py:603-650](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/spaces.py#L603-L650) sits inside the handler for a person's message, and an agent's own reply is written somewhere else \u2014 so agent to agent may not be wired at all. Find out before anything is built on it. A group of agents that can talk to each other is a different product from a group that answers when spoken to. Facts only. #research ^c-a2a**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L74`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3120
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "784fbd2a341fe641",
      "line_num": 75,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**One RAD, two Sage.is instances: raised 2026-08-09. A RAD could be plugged into two AI-UI instances at once, or two instances could share a vault. That makes the RAD a bridge between deployments, not just a resident of one. Nobody has asked for it, and it may be the most valuable thing on this chart. Decide whether it is part of the destination or a later effort. Answer the obvious danger first: two instances writing one vault, with nothing stopping them clashing. #interview ^c-crosslink**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L75`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3210
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "4a3ff43b7cdb79f1",
      "line_num": 76,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**How a RAD gets added: by hand, in Admin \u2192 Settings \u2192 Connections. A group needs a form instead. The likely answer is a Sprig whose settings are the address, the key, the prefix and the display name \u2014 the settings mechanism shipped 2026-08-09, so that part exists. The catalog is hardcoded Python, so this is a code change here. Blocked-by ^c-shape and ^c-many. #interview ^c-added**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L76`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3300
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f93171a7cc90bdab",
      "line_num": 77,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What one actually costs to stand up: facts before anyone argues about preference, and now for both shapes. Inside the container: image size, memory, what the sandbox stops it doing, what happens on restart. As an address: the host, the vault, TLS, issuing a key, how it gets updated, and the monthly bill. Then do the second one in each shape, because the second is what proves the form. #research ^c-cost**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L77`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3390
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "64e506f948ce27a4",
      "line_num": 78,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Who is allowed to talk to a RAD: [spaces.py:350](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/spaces.py#L350) passes `bypass_filter=True`, so a Space agent skips model access control completely ([chat.py:201](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/utils/chat.py#L201)). Anyone in the Space reaches the daemon. For our own agent that is handy. For a client's agent it is the whole question. #interview ^c-speak**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L78`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3480
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "4695b16ad936f693",
      "line_num": 79,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What a reader sees when a RAD is down: [spaces.py:301-303](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/spaces.py#L301-L303) writes a warning to the log and stops. Nothing appears in the Space. A wrong name, a dead daemon and a bad key look exactly the same from the room. `TODO.md:68` has been this since 2026-08-03 and nobody has diagnosed it. #interview ^c-silence**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L79`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3570
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "7f2b8eb34993c053",
      "line_num": 80,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What a RAD is called, in three places at once: the model id, the display name, and the `@mention`. [spaces.py:636-640](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/spaces.py#L636-L640) matches a mention against the name, lowercased, with spaces turned into hyphens. [openai.py:447-454](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/routers/openai.py#L447-L454) puts `prefix_id` on the front of the id, and strips it again on the way out. Two RADs with similar names clash, and the clash is silent. #interview ^c-name**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L80`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3660
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "8e4195be507017a7",
      "line_num": 81,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Where a group's keys live: `PersistentConfig` writes the config blob in the clear ([config.py:204-214](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/config.py#L204-L214) \u2014 nothing in that file encrypts anything). One client is one key in the clear. Ten clients is ten. Decide the limit: only tokens that can be rotated and never a credential to anything else, or the group needs a proper store. An embedded RAD needs no key at all, which is the strongest thing ^c-shape has going for it \u2014 read that card first, because this one may shrink to nothing. #interview ^c-keys**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L81`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3750
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "a4be15eacf8a8945",
      "line_num": 82,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What an operator sees: `/admin/diagnostics` probes tool servers, not model connections. A group needs a list, a health state for each RAD, and a way to tell \"wrong key\" from \"daemon down\" from \"nobody mentioned it\". Put it in the diagnostics page we already have rather than building a second one. #interview ^c-operator**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L82`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3840
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/rad-servers/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "61c7048402b030d8",
      "line_num": 83,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What a RAD can reach on our side: Pullbot writes files inside its own approved directory. The question is what it gets from Sage \u2014 the record, knowledge, uploaded files, or nothing. This is where this chart meets the record. Read [that chart's `^c-space` card](../central-intelligence/TODO.md) before claiming this one, and say which side owns what. #interview ^c-reach**\n`charts/rad-servers/TODO.md:L83`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 3930
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "46df5a780e83751d",
      "line_num": 116,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Where the record lives: pick the substrate and the location in one decision. A Pullbot project directory is now the leading candidate \u2014 it is a folder the agent already writes to, and version history comes free. The other candidates are pb.sage.is, Sage knowledge inside `DATA_DIR`, `models/memories.py`, `models/notes.py`, a synced vault, and a grafted CRM. Two things bite here. The sell-later filter, and the fact that this record holds contact details and commercial terms. #interview ^c-lives**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L116`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4020
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "ec1494d45e636465",
      "line_num": 119,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What the candidate stores give us: get facts on each option before anyone argues preference. For each one answer four questions. What does export and restore look like? Who else can see it? How many hours to stand it up? What does a fifty-row search feel like? Facts only. The choosing is the card above. #research ^c-stores**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L119`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4110
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "67ba0be890aabd02",
      "line_num": 121,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**A second kind of row: the shape holds deals. It does not hold people who will never be a deal. Reviewer T proved it \u2014 four of six fields sat empty, and what he told us had no field at all. `Ss_list` puts the community concierge and the doorman on the same board, and both deal with people like this. Decide: drop them from the record, or add a second row type. #interview ^c-rowtype**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L121`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4200
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "acb74ded2b28a6a2",
      "line_num": 123,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What a rejection looks like: a rejected candidate is not an organization. It is a decision about one, often one we never spoke to. Three questions. Does it get a row or a table line? When it later becomes real, does the line become a row, or do both exist? `Ss_list` says rows retire and never delete \u2014 is a rejection just a retired row that was never alive? #interview ^c-rejection**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L123`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4290
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "5757337b1e144b2e",
      "line_num": 125,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What the agent may not infer: the rule is \"it does not invent a fact nobody wrote down\". That needs teeth. What rule does the agent carry, and how would we catch it breaking? `Ss_list` already bans the prospector from precise money figures for the same reason. A number is a guess wearing a suit. #interview ^c-infer**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L125`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4380
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "6a1d1cd8d5095f49",
      "line_num": 127,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What the agent may change, and what only a human may: the agent edits the record from chat. So decide its limits. Which fields may it set on its own? May it retire a row? May it move a stage without being told to? This card exists because the destination changed on 2026-08-08. #interview ^c-limits**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L127`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4470
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "f2e3cbbe5d2dcf9c",
      "line_num": 129,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**What the widget shows: design the one-glance view. Who has gone quiet. What is owed today. Where the pipeline sits. Design it inside the ui-Sprig limits above. A fragment rendered ahead of time and served flat may beat a live one, and then it needs no script grant at all. Sketch two and react. Three real rows now exist to design against. #prototype ^c-widget**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L129`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4560
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "31e956b42d94aa06",
      "line_num": 131,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The agent in a Space: the mechanism already works. What is left is a real decision. What does the agent know inside a Space? All deals, or that Space's own? Does a Space member see commercial terms? A Space is where a client could one day sit. That is where the sell-later filter meets the sensitivity problem. #interview ^c-space**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L131`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4650
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "54ea944873951a06",
      "line_num": 133,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The shim, or a scheduled job: this card shrank on 2026-08-08 and may not survive. A Resident Agent\u2122 writes files directly, and Pullbot already ships a scheduler with persistent storage. Re-read the card before claiming it. It may fold into [Where the record lives]. One question inside it still stands: can a tool server hold a scoped token instead of a founder's full credential? #interview ^c-shim**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L133`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4740
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "e88faaaf3251fc3b",
      "line_num": 135,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**Reuse the bridge, or ship a second Telegram path: we already ship a Telegram bridge. `BridgeConnection` ([models/bridges.py:17](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/models/bridges.py#L17)) does BotFather tokens, polling or webhook, and a `channel_bridge` mode that links to a Space. Pullbot carries its own Telegram. Decide which one the Sprig's toggle drives. Reusing the bridge gives us the allowlist, the provisioning modes and the kill switch for free. Two paths means two security surfaces, and one of them will rot. Settle the allowlist default here too \u2014 `auto_create` provisioning on a record of commercial terms is wrong. #interview ^c-bridge**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L135`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4830
    },
    {
      "file_path": "charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md",
      "height": 80,
      "id": "c235f32933831a05",
      "line_num": 137,
      "source": "todo_md",
      "tags": [],
      "text": "**The Sprig catalog entry: decide what it declares. Capability slot, delivery kind, and the wires a grafter fills in. Three wires are already known: the Telegram toggle defaulting off, the BotFather token it reveals, and an allowlist of who may speak to it. Existing deliveries are `oci-artifact` for weights, plus the theme and ui fragment paths. A record is neither. The closest precedent is `dev-svelte` \u2014 `capability: \"dev\"`, `server: \"deliver\"`, and \"NOT a running server\" in the comment. The catalog is a hardcoded Python `CATALOG` at [supervisor.py:222](../../app/backend/sage_is_ai/sprigs/supervisor.py#L222), so this is a code change here. One card. The wiring mechanism behind it is under an hour, so it does not need its own. #interview ^c-catalog**\n`charts/central-intelligence/TODO.md:L137`",
      "type": "text",
      "width": 280,
      "x": 360,
      "y": 4920
    },
    {
